  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "commit-message-describes-diff",
      "kind": "noul",
      "ask": "This commit's message ($SUBJECT) claims something the diff does not do, or the diff does something material that the message does not mention.",
      "note": "Judge the message against the diff, not the diff on its own merits. Whether the change is a good one is not the question; whether the message tells the truth about it is. A terse but accurate subject line honours the claim. A message that describes the intent (\"make retries safe\") rather than the mechanism (a new mutex) is accurate when the mechanism serves the intent. \"Refactor\" and \"no behaviour change\" are honoured when every default, exported name, error, and result is the same after as before: moving code, renaming a local or a private field, extracting a private helper, and lifting a literal into a constant of the same value are the refactor, not a change the message had to report. Compare values, not shape: a default whose value changes in the middle of such a diff is the change to report. `diff` may be cut to fit; `stat` and `files` are always complete, so a change visible in `stat` but absent from `diff` did happen.",
      "draft": "f8682af992d5",
      "at": 0.65,
      "state": "bare",
      "subject": "commit",
      "severity": "warning",
      "language": "Git"
    },
    {
      "id": "claim-has-evidence",
      "kind": "noul",
      "ask": "This section ($TITLE) asserts what a player will feel, notice, do, or understand, and nothing in the section backs the assertion with an observation, a measurement, or a cited source.",
      "note": "A measured latency or a model probability is evidence about the system, not about the player; it does not back a claim about what the player feels. A sentence in the form \"if X then the player Y\" that the section itself marks as an intended effect to verify is a hypothesis, not a claim. Judge the section alone; observations recorded in other sections do not count here.",
      "draft": "a10a0dc09472",
      "at": 0.4,
      "state": "bare",
      "subject": "block",
      "severity": "info",
      "language": "Text"
    },
    {
      "id": "companion-does-not-leak",
      "kind": "noul",
      "ask": "This line, shown to the player beside a case, explains the hidden mechanism or states an outcome the player has not observed, instead of pointing at what to compare.",
      "note": "The key name says the role: companion and hints are the voice beside a case; fallback, note, split are fixed replies; L-numbered arrays are hint ladders. Use the surrounding file to tell what the player is looking at: an outcome recorded in the same card's `observation` or `actual`, or on cards the line names, is observed and may be restated. Mentioning a concept (type, order) as something to look at is pointing; asserting that it is the reason, or how the code handles it, is explaining.",
      "draft": "6e1fbad975a0",
      "at": 0.5,
      "state": "located",
      "subject": "node",
      "severity": "warning",
      "language": "TypeScript"
    },
    {
      "id": "jev-question-single-condition",
      "kind": "noul",
      "ask": "This model question bundles two or more independent judgments into one answer, or makes the answer depend on the role a term plays in the text (for example \"counts only when it appears as a condition, not in a rejection clause\").",
      "note": "Judge the question as the model would receive it: `instructions` plus the `criteria` branches. Listing what does NOT count (synonyms that are too weak, a bare mention with no rule) is sharpening, not a second judgment. The defect is a second, independent condition on the same term -- typically a clause about where the term appears (a rejection clause, a different sentence) or what it is being used for. A criterion that requires the text to be about a topic AND to be a proposal or rule (rather than a question or a bare mention) is one judgment -- \"is this a rule about that topic\" -- not two. Ignore the surrounding TypeScript; only the question text matters.",
      "draft": "eaf99b69bfc7",
      "at": 0.62,
      "state": "bare",
      "subject": "node",
      "severity": "warning",
      "language": "TypeScript"
    },
    {
      "id": "llm-never-judges",
      "kind": "noul",
      "ask": "This function ($NAME) lets text generated by a language model decide a game outcome: whether a prediction matched, a verdict, a score, whether the player cleared, which case is shown next, or what the software actually did.",
      "note": "Choosing between the generated text and a fixed fallback text is presentation, not a game outcome. A screening step that asks a separate judgment model whether the generated text leaks facts, and hides it if so, is the intended design and is false. The defect is the generated content itself becoming the answer. Read the helpers in the same file to tell which calls reach a language model.",
      "draft": "7cc823a1d4f4",
      "at": 0.6,
      "state": "located",
      "subject": "node",
      "severity": "warning",
      "language": "TypeScript"
